<description>
Repeat the single colored border segment around the rectangular border, leaving one segment-length gap between copies.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- There is exactly one foreground color.
- All foreground pixels form one contiguous segment on the rectangular border, possibly crossing the cycle start.

Rule:
- Unwrap the grid border clockwise into a cyclic path.
- Let n be the length of the foreground segment.
- Copy the segment with the same color every 2*n border positions around the cycle.

Key insight:
- The foreground segment is not extended by rows or columns directly; it is a motif on the perimeter cycle, repeated with an equal-sized gap.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "single contiguous foreground segment on the rectangular border",
  "path": "cyclic traversal of the border",
  "trajectory": "copy the segment every two segment lengths around the cycle",
  "transform": "leave non-copied border positions and background unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 3-10 rows x 3-15 cols; interior (non-border cells) is all bg
  colors: exactly 2 — one bg color, one fg color
  bg: always 8 [observed]; fg from {1-9}\{bg}
  border_cycle: clockwise perimeter traversal (top-L-to-R, right-T-to-B, bottom-R-to-L, left-B-to-T)
  segment:
    definition: single contiguous run of fg pixels on the border cycle
    length: 2-5 (eval), up to perimeter/4 in general
    position: anywhere on the cycle; may wrap around a corner (1/4 examples do)
  divisibility: perimeter % (2 * seg_len) == 0 — required by solver assert
  n_copies: perimeter / (2 * seg_len), range 2-11; eval range 3-7
  output:
    same grid shape; interior unchanged (all bg)
    segment is stamped every 2*seg_len positions around the border cycle starting from the original segment start
    total fg pixels in output = seg_len * n_copies

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (build the tiled border, then strip copies to get input)
  steps:
    1. Pick H in [3,10], W in [3,15]. Compute perimeter = 2H+2W-4.
    2. Enumerate divisors d of perimeter where d is even and d >= 4.
       Set seg_len = d/2, n_copies = perimeter/d. Require n_copies >= 2.
       Pick one valid (seg_len, n_copies) pair.
    3. Pick a random start position on the border cycle in [0, perimeter).
    4. Build the output grid — fill bg everywhere, then paint fg at positions
       start + k*2*seg_len + j (mod perimeter) for each copy k and offset j in [0, seg_len).
    5. Derive the input by keeping only the first copy (offsets [start, start+seg_len))
       and setting all other border fg pixels back to bg.
    6. Pick bg=8 [observed] or any color; pick fg != bg.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

